package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Five original ten-part stories with character-led mysteries and resolved endings. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch14 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries("the_station_of_small_promises", "The Station of Small Promises", "A student reporter follows a trail of unsigned promises through a railway town.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1, "The Notice in the Rain", listOf("Mira was helping her uncle clear the waiting room at Northbridge Station when she found a notice pinned behind an old timetable. It listed five promises: repair the clock, reopen the reading corner, paint the footbridge, return a lost photograph, and plant a tree. None had a date or signature. Someone had added one sentence at the bottom: 'If you find this, finish what we started.'", "Mira showed the notice to her friend Femi, who ran the school newsletter. They decided to investigate before the station's planned renovation erased the old rooms. Their first clue was a faded stamp: Northbridge Youth Committee, 2004. The committee had disappeared from every recent record.")),
            StoryPart(2, "The Clockmaker's Receipt", listOf("The station clock had been five minutes slow for as long as Mira could remember. A repair receipt tucked into its wooden case named a clockmaker who had retired to the next town. He remembered the youth committee clearly: they had raised money to make the station kinder for people waiting for late trains.", "He also remembered that the clock had been repaired twice, but the second payment was never collected. He gave Mira a small envelope addressed to 'whoever still cares about this place.' Inside was a photograph of five teenagers standing beneath the clock, each holding a different tool.")),
            StoryPart(3, "The Reading Corner", listOf("Behind a stack of unused chairs, Mira found a shelf outline on the wall and a list of donated books. The station manager said the books had been removed after a leak, but nobody knew where they went. Femi noticed that every title on the list had a library stamp from the school across the road.", "The school librarian checked the old accession book and found the missing collection stored in a dry cupboard. A note from the youth committee asked that the books be returned whenever the station had a safe room again. The librarian offered to help rebuild the corner, but wanted to know who would look after it.")),
            StoryPart(4, "Five Names", listOf("The photograph showed five names written on the back: Bisi, Tunde, Amina, Chuka, and Peter. Mira traced them through the town directory. Two had moved away, one worked at the market, and one was now a nurse at the local clinic. The fifth name had been crossed out and replaced with a question mark.", "At the market, Bisi recognised the notice immediately. She explained that the group had stopped meeting after a disagreement about how to spend the money they raised. Nobody had stolen it; they had simply grown busy, and the unfinished list became an embarrassment. She gave Mira the address of Tunde, who had kept the committee's notebook.")),
            StoryPart(5, "The Notebook", listOf("Tunde's notebook contained sketches for the footbridge, receipts for paint, and a record of the tree they hoped to plant. The money had been placed in a community account, but the account was closed when the bank merged with another branch. A final entry said the balance had been transferred to the town improvement fund.", "Mira visited the council office with Femi and asked for the transfer record. The clerk found it after checking the archived files. The money was still accounted for, but the original purpose had been forgotten. The council agreed to release it if residents formed a new volunteer group and submitted a practical plan.")),
            StoryPart(6, "The Photograph", listOf("The lost photograph was the hardest promise. Peter, the crossed-out name, had taken it and left town after his family moved suddenly. A message to his old address was returned, but a former teacher remembered that he had become a photographer and sometimes exhibited at the civic centre.", "Mira found him arranging prints for a local exhibition. When she showed him the picture, he was surprised that anyone had kept looking. He explained that the photograph belonged to Amina, who had been away on the day it was taken. He had promised to give her a copy, then lost contact. He still had the negative.")),
            StoryPart(7, "The Bridge Plan", listOf("Amina returned to Northbridge for a family visit and joined the group at the station. She was now an engineer and offered to inspect the footbridge before anyone began painting. Her report found loose boards and poor lighting, so the volunteers changed their plan: safety repairs first, decoration later.", "The council arranged a proper inspection and approved the repairs. Mira learned that finishing a promise did not mean following an old plan blindly. It meant understanding what people needed now. The group added a new promise to their list: make the station accessible to everyone.")),
            StoryPart(8, "The Tree That Waited", listOf("Chuka, now a gardener, returned with a map showing where the youth committee had once planned to plant a tree. The spot was too close to a drain, so he suggested a young shade tree near the station entrance instead. The school gardening club offered to water it during the holidays.", "The reading corner reopened with donated books, sturdy shelves, and a rota of volunteers. The station manager provided a noticeboard for updates. For the first time, the five old committee members sat together again, not to argue about what had gone wrong but to decide what they could do next.")),
            StoryPart(9, "A Promise in Public", listOf("Mira and Femi published the story in the school newsletter. They included the account records, the bridge inspection, and the names of everyone who helped. Some residents came forward with tools and spare paint; others offered to read with younger children while trains were delayed.", "At a small station meeting, the council confirmed the repairs and agreed to maintain the reading corner. Peter gave Amina the photograph, finally keeping his promise. She placed it in a frame beside the original notice, with all five names written clearly underneath.")),
            StoryPart(10, "The New Timetable", listOf("The station clock was reset, the footbridge repaired, and the young tree planted. The reading corner became a regular stop for commuters and students. Mira kept the old notice in a display case, but added a new page beneath it showing the promises completed and the people who had made them possible.", "On the morning the new timetable went up, Mira found a fresh note on the board. It read: 'Promise six: keep making room for one another.' This time it had several signatures. Mira smiled at the simple truth of it: a town was not repaired by one grand gesture, but by people returning to the small things they had said they would do." ))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_courtyard_code", "The Courtyard Code", "A quiet apartment courtyard hides the story of the neighbours who built it.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1, "The Chalk Squares", listOf("When Kalu moved into Block C, he found a courtyard divided into chalk squares. Each square held a symbol: a cup, a leaf, a key, a book, or a tiny sun. The caretaker said children had drawn them years ago, but nobody remembered why. Kalu liked puzzles and began copying the symbols into his notebook.", "His neighbour Sade recognised one symbol from a box of old photographs in her grandmother's flat. The cup appeared on a picture of the courtyard before the apartment blocks were built. Behind the photo someone had written: 'Start where everyone can sit.'")),
            StoryPart(2, "The Bench with No Name", listOf("The only bench in the courtyard had no plaque and one repaired leg. Kalu and Sade lifted its loose wooden slat and found a folded plan showing a garden, a play area, and a shared kitchen. The drawing was signed by residents from every building, but the date had faded.", "A retired caretaker named Mr Eze studied the plan. He remembered a neighbourhood committee that had created the courtyard together. The symbols were their way of marking places where people had offered help. The cup meant shared meals; the leaf meant gardening; the key meant lending tools.")),
            StoryPart(3, "The Garden Ledger", listOf("The garden square was now covered by paving stones. Mr Eze recalled that a ledger had recorded which families donated seeds and which children watered the plants. Sade found it in a storage cupboard, wrapped in a plastic sheet. The final pages showed that the garden had been closed after a water pipe broke.", "The ledger also listed a small reserve fund for repairs. The building association had never spent it because the account name had been changed during a management handover. Kalu offered to help trace the records, while Sade asked the current caretaker to join their search.")),
            StoryPart(4, "The Key Symbol", listOf("The key symbol led to a locked cabinet in the old laundry room. The caretaker opened it and found hand tools, seed packets, and a handwritten lending register. Residents had borrowed tools for repairs and returned them with notes about what they fixed.", "One note mentioned a missing set of garden plans kept by Mrs Danjuma, a former committee organiser. Her daughter lived nearby and agreed to look through the family papers. She found the plans and a letter explaining that the courtyard had been designed to make neighbours less isolated.")),
            StoryPart(5, "The Shared Kitchen", listOf("The cup symbol pointed to a small room that had once been a shared kitchen. It was now used for storing broken furniture. The building association said it could not reopen without a safety inspection, but several residents volunteered to clear it and make a list of repairs.", "Kalu discovered that the old committee had used the kitchen to organise meals for elderly residents during a long power outage. The story changed how he saw the courtyard: it was not just a shortcut between buildings, but a place built around looking after one another.")),
            StoryPart(6, "A Difficult Meeting", listOf("At the association meeting, some residents objected to spending money on shared spaces. They wanted the reserve fund used for repainting the entrance instead. Sade listened, then presented the ledger and a simple budget separating urgent safety repairs from optional improvements.", "Mr Eze explained how the original committee had asked everyone to contribute in a way they could manage. The association agreed to a trial: reopen one safe room, restore a small planting area, and review the costs after three months. Kalu volunteered to keep the records public.")),
            StoryPart(7, "The Sun Square", listOf("The sun symbol marked a patch of courtyard that received morning light. A local horticulture teacher helped the children choose hardy plants and build raised beds. They discovered that the old water line could be repaired without replacing the entire pipe, saving much of the reserve.", "As the first seedlings went in, residents began leaving notes on the noticeboard: spare pots available, can help on Saturdays, happy to teach chess. The courtyard was becoming a meeting place again, but Kalu wondered why the symbols had been left in chalk instead of painted permanently.")),
            StoryPart(8, "The Last Photograph", listOf("Sade's grandmother found the final photograph in a family album. It showed the original committee standing around the courtyard, each holding a chalk board with one of the symbols. On the back was a message: 'Let the next children decide what comes after us.'", "The group understood that the code was not a secret with one fixed answer. It was an invitation to continue the work. Kalu suggested adding a new symbol for learning, and the children chose an open book. They painted the symbols on weatherproof tiles with permission from the association.")),
            StoryPart(9, "An Open Door", listOf("The shared room passed inspection after the required repairs. Residents donated clean utensils and folding tables, and the association created clear rules for booking and cleaning it. The first gathering was a courtyard history afternoon, where older neighbours told stories and younger ones displayed drawings.", "The reserve fund was documented, the lending cabinet reopened, and the garden beds were assigned to volunteer teams. The association agreed to review the project openly each quarter. Even the residents who had opposed it admitted that the courtyard felt safer and friendlier with more people using it.")),
            StoryPart(10, "The Next Symbol", listOf("At the end of the trial, the association voted to keep the shared spaces and include their maintenance in the annual plan. Kalu and Sade placed the original photographs beside the new symbol tiles, making sure the names of the first committee were recorded.", "A younger child asked what symbol should come next. Kalu handed over the chalk and said it was not his decision. The child drew a small bridge between two squares. Everyone understood: the courtyard code had never been about solving a puzzle and putting it away. It was about finding new ways to connect." ))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_radio_that_remembered", "The Radio That Remembered", "A community radio team uncovers an overlooked local history while saving its station.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1, "Static at Noon", listOf("The community radio station had one week before its funding review. Its programmes had become repetitive, its audience was shrinking, and the old studio radio produced a burst of static every day at noon. Nneka, a student volunteer, was asked to catalogue the equipment before the station closed.", "While testing the radio, she heard a faint recording beneath the static: a woman reading names and dates. The sound lasted only twelve seconds. Nneka told the station manager, who said the radio had not been connected to the archive system in years.")),
            StoryPart(2, "The Voice on Tape", listOf("Engineer Malik opened the radio casing and found a tiny memory card fitted by a previous technician. The file was damaged, but he recovered a clear phrase: 'The bridge opened because the whole street worked together.' Nneka searched the station's archive and found no programme with that title.", "A retired presenter, Auntie Rose, recognised the voice as that of Mrs Afolayan, a local teacher who had recorded oral histories. Her tapes were believed to have been donated to the town library. The station had never received copies.")),
            StoryPart(3, "The Library Catalogue", listOf("The library catalogue listed twelve tapes under Mrs Afolayan's name, but only eleven were on the shelf. The missing tape had been logged as 'Street Voices, final edit.' The librarian remembered lending the box to a history group years earlier and never receiving the last item back.", "Nneka interviewed the group coordinator, who found an old sign-out sheet. The tape had gone to a community centre that was now a sports hall. The caretaker agreed to search its storage room, where boxes of event materials were stacked behind folded chairs.")),
            StoryPart(4, "The Unfinished Programme", listOf("The tape was inside a labelled envelope, but its reel had snapped. Malik repaired it carefully and transferred the audio to a digital file. Mrs Afolayan's recording described residents building a temporary footbridge after floods cut off the market road. The official record credited the council alone.", "The recording included interviews with a carpenter, a nurse, a shopkeeper, and schoolchildren who carried supplies. Nneka realised the story mattered because the people who did the work had never been named. She proposed a new radio series to verify the account and share it.")),
            StoryPart(5, "The Carpenter's Sketch", listOf("The carpenter's name led them to Mr Nwosu, now in his eighties. He still had a sketch of the temporary bridge, with measurements and a list of donated materials. He explained that the council had supplied timber, but residents had designed the safe route and organised shifts.", "He also remembered a disagreement: the original recording had been edited to fit a short broadcast, and several voices were cut. Mrs Afolayan had insisted that the full version be preserved. Nneka asked whether he would speak on air, and he agreed if the others could be found.")),
            StoryPart(6, "Four More Voices", listOf("Nneka and Malik traced the nurse, shopkeeper, and two former students. Each remembered a different part of the work. One had kept a list of households that delivered food; another had photographs of the bridge being tested before it opened.", "Their accounts matched the sketch and the recording. The station manager approved a longer programme, but warned that the funding panel wanted evidence of audience interest. Nneka suggested inviting listeners to send questions and memories before the broadcast.")),
            StoryPart(7, "The Listener Letters", listOf("Letters arrived from people who had been children during the floods. Some remembered carrying drinking water; others remembered neighbours sharing lanterns and dry clothes. One letter included a newspaper clipping that confirmed the bridge had opened two days before the council's official ceremony.", "The evidence was checked and organised into a timeline. Malik cleaned the audio without changing the speakers' words, and Auntie Rose helped identify each voice. The station announced a live programme, with a promise to correct the archive if new facts emerged.")),
            StoryPart(8, "Broadcast Night", listOf("The programme began with the original twelve-second recording, then played the full interviews. Mr Nwosu described the bridge design, the nurse explained the supply route, and the former students read names from the volunteer list. Listeners called in with additional photographs and dates.", "A caller challenged one detail about the opening day. Nneka did not dismiss the correction; she asked the caller to send the document. The station promised to update the timeline after checking it. The manager later said the programme had more listeners than any show that year.")),
            StoryPart(9, "The Corrected Archive", listOf("The caller's document showed that the bridge had been inspected a day earlier than the team first believed. Nneka corrected the timeline and added the source note. The town library agreed to preserve the recordings, photographs, and sketches together, with permission from everyone involved.", "The funding panel praised the station for useful local reporting and strong community participation. It approved another year of support, conditional on a regular archive programme. The manager asked Nneka to help train new student volunteers in careful interviewing and fact-checking.")),
            StoryPart(10, "A Clear Signal", listOf("The radio no longer burst into static at noon; Malik replaced the faulty connection and kept the recovered recording safely backed up. The station launched 'Voices We Keep,' a weekly programme about overlooked local history, with a public correction process for listeners.", "Nneka's first catalogue entry listed every speaker, every source, and every uncertainty still being checked. She understood that remembering was not simply replaying old sounds. It meant listening closely, checking the details, and making sure the people who shaped a place were not left out of its story." ))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_blue_roof_observatory", "The Blue Roof Observatory", "Three classmates turn an abandoned rooftop into a place for science and community discovery.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1, "A Patch of Blue", listOf("Ayo noticed the blue roof while waiting for his bus. The old municipal building had been closed for years, but a narrow staircase led to a rooftop platform with a clear view of the sky. He imagined a telescope there, though the roof was dusty and the door was locked.", "His classmates Lami and David were preparing a science project about light pollution. They asked the building caretaker for permission to inspect the roof. He agreed to show them around, but warned that the structure needed an engineer's approval before anyone could use it.")),
            StoryPart(2, "The Old Star Chart", listOf("In a cupboard near the stairs, the students found a star chart drawn by a former science teacher. It marked the best months to observe bright planets and included notes about nearby streetlights. The chart was dated twenty years earlier and signed by a club called Sky Watch.", "The caretaker remembered the club but said it had ended when the teacher moved away. Ayo found an address in the chart's margin. It belonged to a retired teacher, Mr Lawal, who still lived across town.")),
            StoryPart(3, "A Teacher's Condition", listOf("Mr Lawal was delighted to see the chart, but he would not hand over the club's old telescope immediately. He wanted the students to prove they understood observation safety, accurate records, and the difference between what they saw and what they guessed.", "He gave them a notebook containing simple sky maps and asked them to record the moon's position for two weeks from a safe public viewing spot. The assignment sounded easy until clouds and changing weather made their notes inconsistent.")),
            StoryPart(4, "Cloudy Evidence", listOf("The students learned to record when they could not observe instead of filling gaps with guesses. They added the time, weather, viewing location, and direction to every entry. Their science teacher praised the careful notes and helped them compare observations with a reliable astronomy guide.", "When they returned to Mr Lawal, he checked the notebook and smiled. He agreed to help them apply for a supervised rooftop inspection. The students had learned that good science was not about always having an answer; it was about being honest about what the evidence could show.")),
            StoryPart(5, "The Engineer's Visit", listOf("A structural engineer inspected the roof and found that one corner needed repair before it could hold equipment. The students adjusted their plan and asked the municipality to approve the work. They also proposed a lightweight observation platform and strict adult supervision for public events.", "The municipality agreed to fund basic repairs if the project included free school visits and a maintenance plan. Lami created a budget, David designed a weatherproof logbook, and Ayo contacted local science teachers to ask for volunteers.")),
            StoryPart(6, "The Missing Lens", listOf("Mr Lawal located the old telescope, but its lens was missing. A note in the storage record said it had been sent for cleaning to a shop that had since closed. The students searched the club's photographs and found the shop name reflected in a window behind the telescope.", "The shop's former owner now repaired camera equipment from a small workshop. He remembered the lens and had kept it in a labelled case after the club stopped meeting. He checked it, confirmed it was still usable, and offered to help align the telescope after the roof repairs.")),
            StoryPart(7, "A Light Map", listOf("While waiting for the roof work, the students mapped streetlights around the building. They discovered that one lamp shone directly toward the platform and made faint stars difficult to see. Rather than asking for the lamp to be switched off, they proposed a shield that would direct light onto the pavement.", "The municipal lighting team tested a shield and found it improved visibility for pedestrians while reducing glare toward the roof. The students added the result to their project, showing that science could improve both public safety and the quality of observation.")),
            StoryPart(8, "The First Viewing", listOf("After the repairs passed inspection, Mr Lawal and the science teachers supervised a small evening viewing. The telescope was set up on the approved platform, and visitors took turns looking at the moon. David kept a record of the time and conditions while Lami explained why the view changed through the month.", "A younger visitor asked whether the telescope could show every star in the universe. Ayo explained that it could not, but it could help people ask better questions. The answer became the project's motto: start with what you can observe, then keep learning.")),
            StoryPart(9, "The Weather Test", listOf("The first public science evening was nearly cancelled by clouds. Instead of pretending the sky was clear, the team moved indoors and held a workshop on moon phases, meteorites, and how telescopes work. Attendance was larger than expected because families appreciated having an alternative programme.", "The municipality approved a regular monthly schedule. The students trained younger volunteers to keep logs, check equipment, and explain uncertainty. Mr Lawal donated the star chart to the observatory, with its history displayed beside the telescope.")),
            StoryPart(10, "A Roof for Everyone", listOf("The Blue Roof Observatory opened as a supervised community science space. Schools booked visits, families attended monthly sky nights, and the lighting team used the students' map when planning improvements elsewhere. The old building had become useful again without losing its history.", "Ayo added the final entry to the logbook: 'Tonight we saw the moon clearly. We did not see everything, and that is why we will return.' The three classmates looked across the rooftops and understood that discovery was not a prize waiting at the end of a project. It was a habit they had built together." ))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_missing_market_clock", "The Missing Market Clock", "A market's vanished clock leads a young organiser to uncover the truth behind a long-running rivalry.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1, "The Empty Bracket", listOf("The central market had always been known for its tall clock, which helped traders open their stalls before sunrise. One Monday, the clock was gone. Only four bolts and a pale circle on the wall remained. Traders blamed one another, and the market association threatened to close the main aisle until it was returned.", "Tari, whose aunt sold fabric nearby, noticed a fresh scratch on the wall and a small brass screw on the ground. She asked the market cleaner what happened, but he had only seen a covered handcart leaving before dawn. Tari decided to find out what had happened before the argument grew worse.")),
            StoryPart(2, "Two Competing Stories", listOf("The fabric sellers believed the food traders had moved the clock to make their section more prominent. The food traders insisted the fabric sellers had complained about its noise. Tari interviewed both groups and found that each story relied on something heard second-hand.", "Her aunt advised her not to turn the investigation into another accusation. Tari made a list of verifiable facts: when the clock was last seen, who had keys to the maintenance room, and whether anyone had requested a repair. The association secretary agreed to check the records.")),
            StoryPart(3, "The Repair Request", listOf("The records showed a repair request from six months earlier. The clock had been losing time, and its mounting bracket was cracked. A technician had recommended taking it down temporarily, but the association had postponed the work because members could not agree on who should pay.", "Tari found the technician's contact on the old invoice. He remembered returning to the market the previous week after receiving an urgent call. He had removed the clock for safety, but the person who called him had not told the association where it would be stored.")),
            StoryPart(4, "The Covered Cart", listOf("The cleaner remembered the technician's cart and the workshop logo on its side. Tari and the secretary visited the workshop, where they found the clock wrapped in cloth. The technician showed them the cracked bracket and explained that it could have fallen if left in place.", "He had sent an estimate to the association but received no reply. He had stored the clock safely while waiting for instructions. Tari realised the mystery was not theft at all; it was a maintenance problem made worse by poor communication.")),
            StoryPart(5, "The Old Photograph", listOf("The association wanted to reinstall the clock quickly, but the technician warned that the wall needed reinforcement. Tari searched the market archive for the original mounting plan. An old photograph showed the clock being installed by traders from both sections, standing together beneath a banner.", "On the back, someone had written that the clock was paid for through a shared contribution after a fire damaged the market years earlier. Tari showed the photograph at the next meeting. The traders began to remember how the clock had once represented cooperation rather than ownership.")),
            StoryPart(6, "The Cost of Repair", listOf("The repair estimate was higher than expected. Some traders wanted the municipality to pay, while others argued that the association should use its maintenance fund. Tari asked the treasurer to present the fund records so everyone could see the available amount.", "The records showed enough for the bracket and wall reinforcement, but not for a full replacement mechanism. The technician offered to service the existing clock first and provide a warranty. The traders voted to share the remaining cost according to stall size, with a clear written record.")),
            StoryPart(7, "A Clock Without a Face", listOf("During the repair, the technician discovered that the clock face had been repainted several times, hiding the original maker's mark. The market archive identified the maker as a small workshop that had closed decades ago. A retired apprentice still lived nearby and offered to restore the lettering.", "The apprentice explained that the clock had been built to be readable from both ends of the aisle. The original design included a simple weather cover that had been removed during an earlier renovation. The team decided to restore the useful original features rather than add unnecessary decoration.")),
            StoryPart(8, "The Reopening Dispute", listOf("Even after the repair was complete, the traders argued over which section should host the reopening ceremony. Tari suggested that the clock be unveiled at the centre aisle, where both groups met. She also proposed inviting the cleaner and technician, whose work had been overlooked.", "The association accepted the plan. Each section contributed refreshments, and the market children prepared a short display about the clock's history. The argument that had begun with suspicion was turning into a shared event.")),
            StoryPart(9, "Seven Minutes Early", listOf("On the morning of the ceremony, the clock ran seven minutes fast. Tari noticed before the crowd arrived and called the technician. He adjusted the mechanism and explained how temperature had affected the newly serviced movement.", "The traders laughed at the near-disaster, but Tari insisted that the maintenance instructions be placed in the association office. They created a simple monthly check rota and named two people from different sections to sign each inspection.")),
            StoryPart(10, "Time Shared", listOf("The market clock returned to its place with the original maker's mark restored and a small plaque explaining its shared history. The association published the repair costs and the new maintenance rota, making it clear how the work had been done.", "Tari's aunt said the clock now meant more than arriving on time. It reminded traders that shared places needed shared care. Tari kept the brass screw that had started her investigation, not as a trophy but as a reminder to ask questions before deciding who was to blame." ))
        ))
    )
}
